\title{Countably infinite sets}
\date{2026-03-12T13:58:26Z}
\tag{discrete}
\taxon{Remark}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{For [countably infinite sets](00AT), the situation looks the same, but there is no endpoint:} ##{\begin{array}{cccccc}
  A: & a_0 & a_1 & a_2 & a_3 & \dots \\
    & \updownarrow& \updownarrow& \updownarrow& \updownarrow& \updownarrow \\
  \N: & 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 & \dots
\end{array}}
\p{We can think of this scenario in several equivalent ways:
\ul{
  \li{\p{#{A} is countable if it has a bijection to (a subset of) #{\N}. (definition)}}
  \li{\p{#{A} is countable if its elements can be put in a list. (We can always number the elements of a list #{0, 1, 2, \dots}.)}}
  \li{\p{#{A} is countable if we can write (or imagine) a program that prints the elements of #{A} one after another, in such a way that any given element of #{A} will eventually be printed after some finite amount of time (if we are willing to wait long enough).}}
}
}

\transclude{00AX}
\transclude{00AY}
\transclude{00AZ}
